    thinking: "**Analyzing Bubble Interaction**\n\nI\'m currently focused on `ScreenCaptureService.kt`, specifically lines 192-225. I\'ve noted the code\'s temporary hiding of the floating bubble (setting alpha to 0) to avoid obstructing the screen capture. This observation gives me a point to explore further and examine the logic behind temporarily hiding elements during capture.\n\n\n**Investigating Image Acquisition**\n\nI\'m now digging into `captureCurrentFrame()`\'s interaction with `acquireLatestImage()`. I\'m wondering if the acquired image *immediately* reflects the frame where the bubble\'s visibility was temporarily altered. I\'m focusing specifically on how the UI buffer updates and whether there\'s a delay that needs to be accounted for. The `Thread.sleep(40L)` call is interesting in this context, I\'m trying to figure out if it is related.\n\n\n**Refining Frame Synchronization**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the timing of frame rendering in relation to bubble hiding. It seems `acquireLatestImage()`\'s return value heavily depends on whether SurfaceFlinger has enough time to render a new frame. I\'m considering a solution: hiding the bubble, waiting 60-80ms to ensure a new frame is drawn without the overlay, acquiring the image, and then immediately restoring the bubble\'s visibility. This way, we ensure the captured frame always reflects the intended state, even with the bubble\'s momentary transparency. The crucial `onTemporarilyHideBubble` also needs validation.\n\n\n"
