  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating Rhino Limits**\n\nI\'m currently looking into Rhino\'s instruction limits. I\'ve noted options such as `Context.instructionObserverThreshold`, `contextFactory`, and listeners. I\'ve also observed the potential for setting `context.instructionObserverThreshold` to a specific value, like 100.\n\n\n**Implementing ContextFactory Solution**\n\nI\'ve made significant headway with the `ContextFactory` approach. I\'ve created a custom `CancellableContextFactory` that overrides `observeInstructionCount`. Now, every 100 bytecode instructions, I check the `ExecutionStatus`. If the status is idle, I throw an exception, allowing graceful termination of running scripts. This handles infinite loops, even without API calls or sleep commands. This solution seems robust.\n\n\n**Refining Termination Strategies**\n\nI\'m now refining the termination strategies. The focus is now on ensuring rapid script termination. My analysis includes examining the behavior within `Thread.sleep()`. I\'ve confirmed that `checkCancelled()` will catch cancellation events within 50ms, triggering an immediate exception. Furthermore, I\'ve integrated `activeThread?.interrupt()` into `ExecutionManager.stop()`, alongside `activeJob?.cancel()`, to handle potential sleep states effectively. I\'m satisfied that this comprehensive approach ensures scripts can be stopped promptly.\n\n\n**Designing Script Switching**\n\nI\'m now designing the user interface for script switching within the floating control service. I\'m focusing on adding a \"\304\220\341\273\225i script\" button to the expanded toolbar, which will trigger an overlay window. This window will display a list of available scripts fetched from the database, allowing users to select an active script. I\'m considering using a badge to indicate the currently active script in the list.\n\n\n**Designing Script Switching**\n\nI\'ve finalized the script-switching user interface. The toolbar now includes an \"\304\220\341\273\225i script\" button, which opens an overlay displaying available scripts fetched from the database. When the user selects a script, the toolbar text updates, a \"\304\220\303\243 ch\341\273\215n script: [T\303\252n script]\" toast appears, and the overlay closes. There\'s also a \"\304\220\303\263ng\" button to cancel the operation. This design is highly user-friendly. I\'ve also addressed Requirement 4. Now, when a script completes, either normally or via `stop()`, the execution status goes to `IDLE`, which turns the floating bubble green with a play icon (`\342\226\266`). A corresponding toast message (\"Script \304\221\303\243 xong\", \"Script \304\221\303\243 d\341\273\253ng\", or an error message) will be shown depending on the outcome.\n\n\n**Verifying Playback Integrity**\n\nI\'ve just validated Requirement 5, ensuring that scripts cannot be re-run concurrently. The `ExecutionManager` now robustly prevents the initiation of new threads when a script is already executing. The `togglePlayPause` function is working as intended, and `startActiveScript` checks and ignores concurrent start requests. I\'ve also verified that the floating bubble\'s play/pause logic is flawless.\n\n\n\n**Addressing OCR Button Concerns**\n\nI\'ve addressed the OCR button issue, specifically concerning screen captures and script execution. I examined how `ScreenCaptureService` interacts with `ScriptRunner`. My analysis shows that the floating bubble\'s visibility is temporarily disabled before a screen capture and re-enabled afterward to prevent OCR misreads, with a brief sleep to ensure UI updates are captured. This is critical for scripts with sleep operations.\n\n\n"
